If the event ends before this window begins, then none of the event samples appear in the next input window:

| | | | | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | | ne+De≤nt¯k+1−Nwin+1⟹[ne,ne+De)∩[nt¯k+1−Nwin+1,nt¯k+1+1)=∅.\begin{split}n\_{e}+D\_{e}\leq n\_{\bar{t}\_{k+1}}&-N\_{\text{win}}+1\quad\Longrightarrow\\ [n\_{e},\,n\_{e}+D\_{e})&\cap[n\_{\bar{t}\_{k+1}}-N\_{\text{win}}+1,\,n\_{\bar{t}\_{k+1}}+1)=\emptyset.\end{split} | | (7) |

In other words, the acoustic event terminates strictly before the new observation window begins. In this case, a window-only interface provides no direct evidence that the event occurred. This motivates the necessity of maintaining a causal memory state that persists across decision gaps so that short acoustic cues occurring during (tk−1,tk)(t\_{k-1},t\_{k}) remain available at the next decision.

