### 4.1 Architectural Overview

We follow the VSLA observation interface in Eq. ([4](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2603.16086#S3.E4 "In 3.1.2 Observation Interface ‣ 3.1 VSLA Observation and Action Space ‣ 3 The VSLA Paradigm ‣ Towards the Vision-Sound-Language-Action Paradigm: The HEAR Framework for Sound-Centric Manipulation")). At decision time tkt\_{k}, the freshest sensor data corresponds to the delayed index t¯k\bar{t}\_{k} (Eq. ([2](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2603.16086#S3.E2 "In 3.1.2 Observation Interface ‣ 3.1 VSLA Observation and Action Space ‣ 3 The VSLA Paradigm ‣ Towards the Vision-Sound-Language-Action Paradigm: The HEAR Framework for Sound-Centric Manipulation"))), and the per-decision observation is otk=(It¯k1:V,Atk,l,st¯k)o\_{t\_{k}}=(I\_{\bar{t}\_{k}}^{1:V},A\_{t\_{k}},l,s\_{\bar{t}\_{k}}). The audio window AtkA\_{t\_{k}} contains only the most recent TwinT\_{\text{win}} seconds of waveform available by t¯k\bar{t}\_{k} (Eq. ([3](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2603.16086#S3.E3 "In 3.1.2 Observation Interface ‣ 3.1 VSLA Observation and Action Space ‣ 3 The VSLA Paradigm ‣ Towards the Vision-Sound-Language-Action Paradigm: The HEAR Framework for Sound-Centric Manipulation"))). When a cue is brief and the next decision is delayed, the cue can fall outside this window and become unavailable at the next query (Eq. ([7](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2603.16086#S3.E7 "In 3.2.4 Sound Causality and Evidence Vanishing ‣ 3.2 Temporal Mismatch and the Blind Execution Interval ‣ 3 The VSLA Paradigm ‣ Towards the Vision-Sound-Language-Action Paradigm: The HEAR Framework for Sound-Centric Manipulation"))).

