### 4.4 Advancer

Capturing a transient cue is necessary, but long-horizon monitoring also requires a stable notion of time. When vision is quasi-static, a policy that only sees snapshot observations may receive nearly identical inputs across many decisions, even though the desired behavior changes with elapsed time (Eq. ([9](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2603.16086#S3.E9 "In 3.3 Temporal Grounding and Motion Collapse ‣ 3 The VSLA Paradigm ‣ Towards the Vision-Sound-Language-Action Paradigm: The HEAR Framework for Sound-Centric Manipulation"))). The Advancer addresses this by learning audio dynamics through near-future prediction, as shown in Figure [5](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2603.16086#S4.F5 "Figure 5 ‣ 4.4 Advancer ‣ 4 The HEAR Framework ‣ Towards the Vision-Sound-Language-Action Paradigm: The HEAR Framework for Sound-Centric Manipulation"). This predictive learning encourages the shared latent ztkz\_{t\_{k}} to reflect temporal progression, which can improve stability during extended listening phases and sharpen reactivity once a cue occurs.

