##### Microwave.

The Microwave scenario requires the robot to stand by until the appliance emits a brief “ding” at a randomized time, as shown in Figure [8](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2603.16086#S6.F8 "Figure 8 ‣ Microwave. ‣ 6.2.3 Trigger Tasks ‣ 6.2 Simulation on HEAR-Bench ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ Towards the Vision-Sound-Language-Action Paradigm: The HEAR Framework for Sound-Centric Manipulation"). The correct behavior is to open the door only after this transient cue has occurred.

This task is designed to directly stress cue dropout under execution gaps. The acoustic cue can be shorter than the effective perception-action gap GkG\_{k} (Eq. ([5](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2603.16086#S3.E5 "In 3.2.2 Decision Cadence and Latency ‣ 3.2 Temporal Mismatch and the Blind Execution Interval ‣ 3 The VSLA Paradigm ‣ Towards the Vision-Sound-Language-Action Paradigm: The HEAR Framework for Sound-Centric Manipulation"))) and may occur while the policy is not being queried, such as inside the Blind Execution Interval under open-loop chunking (Section [3.2.3](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2603.16086#S3.SS2.SSS3 "3.2.3 Action Chunking and the Blind Execution Interval ‣ 3.2 Temporal Mismatch and the Blind Execution Interval ‣ 3 The VSLA Paradigm ‣ Towards the Vision-Sound-Language-Action Paradigm: The HEAR Framework for Sound-Centric Manipulation")). By the next decision time, the cue may have already vanished from the most recent window AtA\_{t}, making it unobservable to memoryless policies (Eq. ([7](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2603.16086#S3.E7 "In 3.2.4 Sound Causality and Evidence Vanishing ‣ 3.2 Temporal Mismatch and the Blind Execution Interval ‣ 3 The VSLA Paradigm ‣ Towards the Vision-Sound-Language-Action Paradigm: The HEAR Framework for Sound-Centric Manipulation"))).

