#### 6.4.2 Temporal Motion Collapse and Action Continuity

Chunked policies operating over long horizons can suffer from Temporal Motion Collapse, a phenomenon where meaningful movement is concentrated early in a chunk while later steps degenerate into near-identical actions (Intelligence et al., [2025](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2603.16086#bib.bib15 "π0.5: A vision-language-action model with open-world generalization"); Zhao et al., [2023](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2603.16086#bib.bib12 "Learning Fine-Grained Bimanual Manipulation with Low-Cost Hardware")). To quantify this effect, we compute a Cartesian displacement diagnostic for the end-effector: dt≜‖pt+1−pt‖2d\_{t}\triangleq\|p\_{t+1}-p\_{t}\|\_{2}, where ptp\_{t} is the position implied by the commanded joint angles at time tt. We classify a step as “low-motion” if dt<2​mmd\_{t}<2\,\mathrm{mm} and report the fraction of such steps across all executed sequences in Table [6](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2603.16086#S6.T6 "Table 6 ‣ 6.4.1 Component Ablations ‣ 6.4 System Analysis and Ablations ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ Towards the Vision-Sound-Language-Action Paradigm: The HEAR Framework for Sound-Centric Manipulation").

